{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nA rebar client installing from source reported this minor issue alongside the archived-child claim bug (b7a2-4453-3bbd-4a54). Their pipx invocation reported that pipx needs uv>=0.9.17 while the selected host uv was 0.7.18. The failure reads like a rebar packaging problem even though the reported workaround proves it is a host pipx/uv toolchain mismatch.\n\n## Approach\nAdd a troubleshooting callout immediately after the install commands in README.md section `### PyPI — pipx / pip`, the repository’s canonical pipx installation surface. Phrase it conditionally around the observed error rather than asserting that every older uv release breaks every pipx install. Show `pipx install --backend pip \"<path>[agents]\"` as the workaround and state that the mismatch is not a rebar packaging defect. The current local `pipx install --help` confirms `--backend {pip,uv}`; no package metadata or runtime behavior changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The README.md `### PyPI — pipx / pip` section describes the observed `uv>=0.9.17` error; verified with `grep -Fq \"uv>=0.9.17\" README.md`.\n- [ ] The same callout shows `pipx install --backend pip \"<path>[agents]\"`; verified with `grep -Fq -- \"--backend pip\" README.md`.\n- [ ] The callout explicitly identifies the condition as a host pipx/uv toolchain mismatch, not a rebar packaging defect; verified by reading the rendered paragraph in its install-section context.\n- [ ] Repository lint remains green; verified with `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" make lint`."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785556080672808001,"uuid":"9a78dd3a-7ca0-4a23-9e81-222ea61ce42f"}